/* rage128 optional GPU raster backend -- span shading kernel.

   This kernel shades the spans the host captured for one segment and
   must produce the same bytes as the interpreter's pixel loop in
   vid_ati_rage128_3d.c. The helper names follow the C copies of that
   loop in the JIT test harness (the rr_ and ref_ functions in
   tests/video/rage128/jit-harness/jit_host_test.c).

   The source is compiled once, by scripts/regen-r128-gpu-spv.sh. Four
   state axes are Vulkan specialization constants, fixed per pipeline
   at vkCreateComputePipelines time from the r128_gpu_variants table:
     V_TEX   0 = untextured, 1 = one stage, 2 = two stages
     V_LOD   stage 0 computes a LOD (draw state need_lod)
     V_BLEND alpha blending reads the destination (alpha_en)
     V_Z     the depth/stencil cell is live: Z test, stencil, or both
   Everything else is a runtime selector read from the triangle record:
   texel format (every datatype the interpreter decodes, which is all
   but CI16), clamp and border modes, filter select (minb, mag,
   mipdis), the full combine code book for both stages, the auxiliary
   scissor rectangles, Z compare and Z write, the D24S8 stencil test
   (compare, three ops, reference, value and write masks), the blend
   code book, sec_sel and need_lod2, and the destination format
   (ARGB1555, RGB565, ARGB4444 or ARGB8888) with its dither enable and
   plane mask. A folded pipeline (C_FOLD below) pins some of these to
   constants as well.

   Execution model: one workgroup per framebuffer row of one run. The
   host splits a segment into runs of consecutive triangles that share
   a kernel id and a combine tuple. Each run dispatches its own pipeline
   over its own region of the rows and slots tables (pc.rows_base). All
   runs go in one submit, with a pipeline barrier between two runs only
   where their rows or texture byte ranges overlap. A segment has one
   render target, and the host keeps draws whose color and Z rows alias
   each other off the parallel path, so two rows never share bytes and
   the workgroups need no ordering between them.

   Within a row the workgroup walks batches, with a buffer barrier at
   the start of each level (see main()). A batch packs several spans
   into the workgroup's GPU_BATCH_SLOTS lane groups of GPU_SLOT_W lanes
   each: slots[] names the span a group shades and the pixel offset
   into it. With GPU_SLOT_W at 16, a 57 px span takes four groups and
   the rest of the batch holds other spans. A dispatch lasts as long as
   its row with the most serial passes, so packing several spans into
   one pass shortens it without needing more workgroups.

   Batch safety: the host packs spans of one row into one batch only
   when their color byte ranges and their Z byte ranges are disjoint;
   disjoint pixels are not enough, since a 32 bpp and a 16 bpp draw on
   one row can share bytes at different x. A segment flushes on any
   dst_offset, dst_pitch or z_offset change, so drow and zrow depend
   only on py. A word on the boundary between two byte ranges can still
   be shared; the unaligned stores below write it with byte-lane
   atomics. Overlapping spans go to different levels in their original
   order, which keeps draw order. The one exception is the commutative
   stencil class (see s_atomic in pixel()), whose spans may overlap in
   one batch. GPU_SLOT_W is a lane-addressing unit, not an assumption
   about the device's subgroup size: results do not depend on the
   subgroup size, only packing efficiency does.

   Z ladder: the interpreter steps zline += dZdx one pixel at a time in
   double, and zline + i * dZdx computed in parallel would round
   differently. The pre-pass (vid_ati_rage128_gpu_lad.comp) walks each
   span's chain at the head of the segment submit and stores the
   quantized depth per pixel in binding 6; this kernel only reads it.

   Bit-exactness rules:
   - every float local is `precise`, and the host sets
     MVK_CONFIG_FAST_MATH_ENABLED=0; both are needed.
   - no float `/` in the pixel path. Vulkan allows OpFDiv an error of
     2.5 ULP, so a `/` does not give the C result. fdiv_cr() and
     div255() build the correctly rounded IEEE-754 quotient in integer
     math. Add, subtract, multiply and the conversions are correctly
     rounded under the Vulkan precision rules, so with those helpers
     the kernel does not depend on how well the driver rounds.
   - float->int conversions follow the interpreter's explicit rule
     (truncate, saturate, NaN gives 0) on every host; sat_i32 and
     sat_u32 spell it out.
   - the blend wrap quantizer (bfcn bit 0) uses roundEven, the
     round-to-nearest-even of C lrintf.
   - texture reads are masked, then linear, as in the interpreter's
     r3d_texel: the start address wraps through the slot mask and a
     multi-byte read then runs past it without wrapping. The VRAM
     buffer has a 4-byte guard word past vram_size, so a read that
     starts at the top of VRAM stays inside the buffer.

   Relevant literature:

   [1] ATI Technologies, "RAGE 128 PRO Register Reference Guide",
       RRG-G04500-C Rev 1.01, January 2000. Cited below as
       "RRG: <register>, p. <printed page> / PDF <viewer page>".

   [2] ATI Technologies, "RAGE 128 Software Development Guide",
       SDK-G04000 Rev 0.01, August 1999. Cited as "SDK: <section>,
       p. <printed page> / PDF <viewer page>". */
#extension GL_EXT_shader_explicit_arithmetic_types_int64 : require
#extension GL_EXT_shader_16bit_storage : require

layout(constant_id = 0) const int V_TEX   = 1;
layout(constant_id = 1) const int V_LOD   = 1;
layout(constant_id = 2) const int V_BLEND = 0;
layout(constant_id = 3) const int V_Z     = 1;

/* Bench-only knob for the gputri tool (timing only, wrong results).
   The device backend never sets it, so its pipelines fold it to 0.
   B_NOBAR drops the barrier at the start of each batch level. */
layout(constant_id = 5) const int B_NOBAR = 0;
/* B_FOLD is a bench-only bitmask for the gputri tool: each bit pins one
   group of runtime selectors to a fixed value so the compiler can drop
   that branch tree, which shows what folding the group would save.
   Pixels are wrong; timing is representative. Bit 8 is unused: the
   combine selectors fold in the device backend through C_FOLD below. */
#define FB_FMT   1  /* texel format dt + s3tc block class */
#define FB_WRAP  2  /* clamp s/t + border            */
#define FB_FILT  4  /* minb / mag / mipdis           */
#define FB_BLEND 16 /* blend code book               */
#define FB_ZAUX  32 /* zfn, alpha test, aux scissor, sec_sel/need_lod2 */
#define FB_EDGE  64 /* proven signed-32 edge range   */
layout(constant_id = 6) const int B_FOLD = 0;
#define FOLDB(bit, v, c) (((B_FOLD & (bit)) != 0) ? uint(c) : (v))

/* Combine fold, used by the device backend. C_FOLD = 0 keeps the
   runtime per-triangle selectors: the base ("uber") pipelines, which
   also run any draw whose combine tuple did not fit the host's tuple
   table. C_FOLD = 1 pins both stages' combine selectors to the
   constants below, so the compiler drops the unused branches of
   tex_combine in that pipeline. The values are the comb_cfg[] fields
   {comb, fmsb, cfac, ifac, comba, afac, ifaca} for each stage. */
layout(constant_id = 7)  const int C_FOLD   = 0;
layout(constant_id = 8)  const int C_COMB0  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 9)  const int C_FMSB0  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 10) const int C_CFAC0  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 11) const int C_IFAC0  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 12) const int C_COMBA0 = 0;
layout(constant_id = 13) const int C_AFAC0  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 14) const int C_IFACA0 = 0;
layout(constant_id = 15) const int C_COMB1  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 16) const int C_FMSB1  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 17) const int C_CFAC1  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 18) const int C_IFAC1  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 19) const int C_COMBA1 = 0;
layout(constant_id = 20) const int C_AFAC1  = 0;
layout(constant_id = 21) const int C_IFACA1 = 0;
/* stg is a literal at both call sites, so this folds to one constant */
#define FOLDC(stg, k, c0, c1) \
    ((C_FOLD != 0) ? uint((stg) == 0u ? (c0) : (c1)) \
                   : tris[ti].comb_cfg[(stg) * 8u + (k)])
/* Texture-lighting word, carried in stage 0's spare comb_cfg slot:
   TEX_LIGHT_FN (SDK: Texture Mapping, p. 6-46 / PDF 158) in [3:0],
   TEX_CNTL_C bit 6 in [4] and ALPHA_LIGHT_FN (SDK: Texture Mapping,
   p. 6-47 / PDF 159) in [7:5]. A folded pipeline pins it with the
   stage selectors; 0 means no lighting pass. */
layout(constant_id = 29) const int C_LIGHT = 0;
#define FOLDL() ((C_FOLD != 0) ? uint(C_LIGHT) : tris[ti].comb_cfg[7])

/* Stencil fold. The host's combine tuple includes the draw's stencil
   enable, so a folded (C_FOLD = 1) pipeline only runs draws whose
   stencil enable equals S_STEN. Pinning it lets the compiler drop the
   stencil read, compare and update from the stencil-off pipelines,
   which are most of them. The uber pipelines (C_FOLD = 0) read the
   enable at run time. */
layout(constant_id = 22) const int S_STEN = 0;
/* Dead-color fold. A draw with no color effect (write mask 0, no alpha
   test, no chroma key) does no interpolation, texturing, combine,
   blend, dither or color access. Carrying this bit in the tuple gives
   such draws, typically stencil-only passes, their own folded kernel
   without adding an axis to the base kernel table. */
layout(constant_id = 23) const int C_DEAD = 0;
/* Texel-format fold. -1 keeps the runtime st_cfg selector (the uber
   pipelines, and folded pipelines whose tuple leaves that stage's
   format open). A value >= 0 pins the stage's texel datatype or S3TC
   class, so the compiler drops the other cases of the texel() format
   switch in that pipeline. */
layout(constant_id = 25) const int F_DT0   = -1;
layout(constant_id = 26) const int F_S3TC0 = -1;
layout(constant_id = 27) const int F_DT1   = -1;
layout(constant_id = 28) const int F_S3TC1 = -1;
/* stg is a literal after inlining (see FOLDC), so this folds away */
#define FOLDF(stg, c0, c1, k) \
    ((C_FOLD != 0 && ((stg) == 0u ? (c0) : (c1)) >= 0) \
        ? uint((stg) == 0u ? (c0) : (c1)) : cfg(ti, stg, k))

/* Serial execution for self-aliasing draws (color and Z rows that
   alias each other, or a draw that samples its own render target): one
   workgroup, invocation 0 only, spans in capture order and pixels left
   to right. That is the interpreter's order, so a texel read that hits
   a byte an earlier pixel of the same draw stored sees the same value.
   The rows pair holds (first span index, span count) instead. Serial
   pipelines are always uber pipelines (C_FOLD 0). */
layout(constant_id = 24) const int V_SERIAL = 0;

#include "vid_ati_rage128_gpu_f64.glsl"
#include "vid_ati_rage128_gpu_abi.glsl"

layout(local_size_x = GPU_BATCH_SLOTS * GPU_SLOT_W) in;

layout(std430, binding = 0) coherent buffer V { uint16_t vram[]; };
/* Same buffer, word view: texture fetches plus the 4-byte color
   (ARGB8888) and Z (D24S8) cells the u16 view cannot address. It is
   coherent so that stores are visible to the batches after a level
   barrier and to later dispatches. An aligned cell belongs to one
   pixel, so its read-modify-write cannot race another pixel. A
   straddling cell (a DST_OFFSET or Z_OFFSET that is not a multiple of
   the cell size) shares its words with the neighboring cells and goes
   through the byte-lane atomic ust* stores instead. */
layout(std430, binding = 1) coherent buffer W { uint texw[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 2) readonly buffer R { uint rows[]; };  /* pairs: batch base, count */
/* GPU_BATCH_SLOTS words per batch, one per GPU_SLOT_W-lane group:
   0xffffffff = idle, else span index << 8 | pixel offset. The offset is
   a multiple of GPU_SLOT_W and fits 8 bits because the host cuts spans
   at 256 px. Bit 0 of a batch's first word is the level-open flag
   (see main()). */
layout(std430, binding = 3) readonly buffer O { uint slots[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 4) readonly buffer S { seg_span_t spans[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 5) readonly buffer T { seg_tri_t tris[]; };
/* quantized depth per pixel (16 or 24 bits; table-fog draws add the fog
   entry in [31:24] and a second word), written by the pre-pass */
layout(std430, binding = 6) readonly buffer L { uint lad[]; };
/* Imported tex_stage arena, word view: texture levels staged from AGP
   memory. A slot whose packed base has bit 31 set samples here with
   mask-then-add addressing (arena_off + (off & (slice - 1))), the
   interpreter's staged scheme; resident slots use the VRAM
   add-then-mask form. */
layout(std430, binding = 7) readonly buffer SW { uint stagew[]; };
/* CI4/CI8 palette snapshots, 256 ARGB words each; tris[].pal_base picks
   this draw's snapshot */
layout(std430, binding = 8) readonly buffer PB { uint pal[]; };
/* Color and Z render targets staged from AGP memory: the c_stage and
   z_stage arenas, imported without a copy, each with the same u16 and
   word views as VRAM. A span whose drow or zrow has bit 31 set
   addresses the matching arena relative to its start, with no
   vram_mask wrap. That is safe because the host refuses any draw whose
   scissor extent is longer than the staged length. */
layout(std430, binding = 9) coherent buffer CS16 { uint16_t cstg16[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 10) coherent buffer CS32 { uint cstgw[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 11) coherent buffer ZS16 { uint16_t zstg16[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 12) coherent buffer ZS32 { uint zstgw[]; };

layout(push_constant) uniform P { uint vram_mask; uint rows_base; } pc;

/* 4x4 ordered dither matrix
   {0,8,2,10, 12,4,14,6, 3,11,1,9, 15,7,13,5}, packed as nibbles into
   two words. A const array indexed at run time is lowered to a 64-byte
   local copy per loop iteration, which would land on the per-pixel
   path. */
#define BAYER4(i) ((((i) < 8u ? 0x6e4ca280u : 0x5d7f91b3u) >> (((i) & 7u) * 4u)) & 0xfu)

/* The interpreter's float to signed int conversion: truncate toward
   zero, saturate to the int range, NaN gives 0. A GLSL int() of an
   out-of-range value is undefined, so the range is tested first. */
int
sat_i32(float f)
{
    if (!(f > -2147483648.0))
        return f < 0.0 ? -2147483648 : 0; /* NaN fails both compares */
    if (f >= 2147483648.0)
        return 2147483647;
    return int(f);
}

/* The interpreter's float to unsigned conversion: negative and NaN
   give 0, too large saturates. */
uint
sat_u32(float f)
{
    if (!(f > 0.0))
        return 0u;
    if (f >= 4294967296.0)
        return 0xffffffffu;
    return uint(f);
}

#include "vid_ati_rage128_gpu_fdiv.glsl"

/* ---- masked-then-linear loads: the caller masks the address, the read
   itself runs linear past it, into the guard word at the top ---- */

uint
ldu8(uint a)
{
    return bitfieldExtract(texw[a >> 2], int((a & 3u) * 8u), 8);
}

uint
ldu16(uint a)
{
    return ldu8(a) | (ldu8(a + 1u) << 8);
}

uint
ldu32(uint a)
{
    uint w0 = texw[a >> 2];
    uint sh = (a & 3u) * 8u;

    if (sh == 0u)
        return w0;
    return (w0 >> sh) | (texw[(a >> 2) + 1u] << (32u - sh));
}

/* the same loads on the stage arena, whose buffer has the same 4-byte
   guard word past its end as the VRAM buffer */
uint
sldu8(uint a)
{
    return bitfieldExtract(stagew[a >> 2], int((a & 3u) * 8u), 8);
}

uint
sldu16(uint a)
{
    return sldu8(a) | (sldu8(a + 1u) << 8);
}

uint
sldu32(uint a)
{
    uint w0 = stagew[a >> 2];
    uint sh = (a & 3u) * 8u;

    if (sh == 0u)
        return w0;
    return (w0 >> sh) | (stagew[(a >> 2) + 1u] << (32u - sh));
}

/* ---- staged-or-resident cell accessors for the color and Z targets.
   The selector (bit 31 of drow or zrow) is the same for a whole span,
   so every lane of a group takes the same branch. Word cells use the
   arena's word view and u16 cells its u16 view, matching the two VRAM
   views the resident paths use. ---- */

uint
cw_ld(bool s, uint w)
{
    return s ? cstgw[w] : texw[w];
}

void
cw_st(bool s, uint w, uint v)
{
    if (s) cstgw[w] = v; else texw[w] = v;
}

uint
c16_ld(bool s, uint c)
{
    return s ? uint(cstg16[c]) : uint(vram[c]);
}

void
c16_st(bool s, uint c, uint v)
{
    if (s) cstg16[c] = uint16_t(v); else vram[c] = uint16_t(v);
}

uint
zw_ld(bool s, uint w)
{
    return s ? zstgw[w] : texw[w];
}

void
zw_st(bool s, uint w, uint v)
{
    if (s) zstgw[w] = v; else texw[w] = v;
}

uint
zw_cas(bool s, uint w, uint cur, uint nv)
{
    return s ? atomicCompSwap(zstgw[w], cur, nv)
             : atomicCompSwap(texw[w], cur, nv);
}

uint
z16_ld(bool s, uint c)
{
    return s ? uint(zstg16[c]) : uint(vram[c]);
}

void
z16_st(bool s, uint c, uint v)
{
    if (s) zstg16[c] = uint16_t(v); else vram[c] = uint16_t(v);
}

/* ---- unaligned (straddling) cell accessors, resident VRAM only. A
   staged address is relative to the arena start (row * pitch_bytes +
   px * bpp), so it is always cell-aligned; only a DST_OFFSET or
   Z_OFFSET that is not a multiple of the cell size can make a VRAM
   cell straddle its element. Reads assemble bytes through the word
   view like the texture loads (the base is pre-masked and the bytes
   run linear into the guard). Writes must be byte-lane atomic: each
   byte belongs to one pixel, but a straddled word is shared with the
   neighboring cell, so a plain read-modify-write of the word could
   lose the neighbor's concurrent store. The And/Or pair is not atomic
   as a pair, but between the two operations only this cell's bytes are
   cleared, and no other invocation reads them. ---- */

void
ust8(uint a, uint v)
{
    uint sh = (a & 3u) * 8u;

    atomicAnd(texw[a >> 2], ~(0xffu << sh));
    atomicOr(texw[a >> 2], (v & 0xffu) << sh);
}

/* a is odd (aligned cells use the u16 view), so sh is 8 or 24 */
void
ust16(uint a, uint v)
{
    uint w  = a >> 2;
    uint sh = (a & 3u) * 8u;

    if (sh == 24u) {
        atomicAnd(texw[w], 0x00ffffffu);
        atomicOr(texw[w], v << 24);
        atomicAnd(texw[w + 1u], 0xffffff00u);
        atomicOr(texw[w + 1u], (v >> 8) & 0xffu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(texw[w], ~(0xffffu << sh));
        atomicOr(texw[w], (v & 0xffffu) << sh);
    }
}

/* a & 3 nonzero (aligned cells use the word store) -- both shifts < 32 */
void
ust32(uint a, uint v)
{
    uint w  = a >> 2;
    uint sh = (a & 3u) * 8u;

    atomicAnd(texw[w], ~(0xffffffffu << sh));
    atomicOr(texw[w], v << sh);
    atomicAnd(texw[w + 1u], 0xffffffffu << sh);
    atomicOr(texw[w + 1u], v >> (32u - sh));
}

/* texel address: resident wraps base+off through the slot mask (the
   interpreter's vram scheme); staged wraps off within its own slice,
   then offsets into the arena */
uint
taddr(bool stgd, uint base, uint mask, uint boff)
{
    return stgd ? base + (boff & mask) : (base + boff) & mask;
}

uint
tldu8(bool stgd, uint a)
{
    return stgd ? sldu8(a) : ldu8(a);
}

uint
tldu16(bool stgd, uint a)
{
    return stgd ? sldu16(a) : ldu16(a);
}

uint
tldu32(bool stgd, uint a)
{
    return stgd ? sldu32(a) : ldu32(a);
}

uint
lerp_packed(uint x, uint y, uint w)
{
    uint iw = 256u - w;
    uint rb = ((x & 0x00ff00ffu) * iw + (y & 0x00ff00ffu) * w + 0x00800080u) >> 8;
    uint ag = (((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ffu) * iw + ((y >> 8) & 0x00ff00ffu) * w + 0x00800080u) >> 8;

    return ((ag & 0x00ff00ffu) << 8) | (rb & 0x00ff00ffu);
}

/* r3d_texcoord_fx: snap a texel coordinate to a 1/4096 grid before it
   is floored, so float jitter cannot flip the chosen texel */
float
texcoord_fx(float f)
{
    precise float r = roundEven(f * 4096.0) * (1.0 / 4096.0);

    return r;
}

/* r3d_log2f_fast: exponent plus a cubic in the mantissa, the
   interpreter's LOD approximation term for term */
float
log2f_fast(float x)
{
    uint          u = floatBitsToUint(x);
    precise float e = float(int((u >> 23) & 0xffu)) - 127.0;
    precise float m = uintBitsToFloat((u & 0x007fffffu) | 0x3f800000u);
    precise float r = e + (-2.133847707 + m * (3.010783972 + m * (-1.029521946 + m * 0.153918478)));

    return r;
}

/* Compare codes 0 (never) to 7 (always), the same for Z_TEST (SDK:
   Setting 3D Render States, p. 6-55 / PDF 167), STENCIL_TEST (SDK:
   Setting 3D Render States, p. 6-56 / PDF 168) and ALPHA_TEST_OP (SDK:
   Setting 3D Render States, p. 6-51 / PDF 163). a is the left operand
   in the guide's tables: source Z, masked stencil reference, source
   alpha. */
int
cmp_fn(uint fn, uint a, uint b)
{
    switch (fn & 7u) {
        case 0u: return 0;
        case 1u: return a < b ? 1 : 0;
        case 2u: return a <= b ? 1 : 0;
        case 3u: return a == b ? 1 : 0;
        case 4u: return a >= b ? 1 : 0;
        case 5u: return a > b ? 1 : 0;
        case 6u: return a != b ? 1 : 0;
        default: return 1;
    }
}

/* rr_tex_wrap: the four clamp modes, 0 wrap, 1 mirror, 2 clamp, 3
   border (SDK: Texture Mapping, p. 6-41 / PDF 153). Border returns -1
   and the caller substitutes the border color. */
int
tex_wrap(int c, int n, uint mode)
{
    int m;

    switch (mode & 3u) {
        case 0u:
            return c & (n - 1);
        case 1u:
            m = c & (2 * n - 1);
            return (m < n) ? m : (2 * n - 1 - m);
        case 2u:
            return (c < 0) ? 0 : (c >= n) ? n - 1 : c;
        default:
            return (c < 0 || c >= n) ? -1 : c;
    }
}

uint
cfg(int ti, uint stg, uint k)
{
    /* bench-only fixed values, a typical Quake III state: RGB565, no
       S3TC, wrap, bilinear within the nearest mip level (minb 3),
       bilinear magnify, mipmapping on */
    if ((B_FOLD & FB_FMT) != 0 && k < 2u)
        return k == 0u ? 4u : 0u;
    if ((B_FOLD & FB_WRAP) != 0 && k >= 2u && k < 5u)
        return 0u;
    if ((B_FOLD & FB_FILT) != 0 && k >= 5u)
        return k == 5u ? 3u : k == 6u ? 1u : 0u;
    return tris[ti].st_cfg[stg * 8u + k];
}

void
slot_of(int ti, uint stg, int cslot, out uint lw, out uint lh,
        out uint base, out uint mask, out bool stgd)
{
    if (stg == 0u) {
        lw   = tris[ti].slot0[cslot * 4 + 0];
        lh   = tris[ti].slot0[cslot * 4 + 1];
        base = tris[ti].slot0[cslot * 4 + 2];
        mask = tris[ti].slot0[cslot * 4 + 3];
    } else {
        lw   = tris[ti].slot1[cslot * 4 + 0];
        lh   = tris[ti].slot1[cslot * 4 + 1];
        base = tris[ti].slot1[cslot * 4 + 2];
        mask = tris[ti].slot1[cslot * 4 + 3];
    }
    /* bit 31: the level lives in the stage arena; a resident base is a
       VRAM byte offset and never has it set */
    stgd  = (base & 0x80000000u) != 0u;
    base &= 0x7fffffffu;
}

/* YCbCr (video range, BT.601) -> ARGB8888: the interpreter's
   r3d_yuv_to_argb, whose integer matrix (r128_yuv_to_rgb) the overlay
   scaler uses too. All integer math, so it matches exactly. */
uint
yuv_to_argb(uint a, int y, int cb, int cr)
{
    int c = y - 16;
    int d = cb - 128;
    int e = cr - 128;
    int r = (298 * c + 409 * e + 128) >> 8;
    int g = (298 * c - 100 * d - 208 * e + 128) >> 8;
    int b = (298 * c + 516 * d + 128) >> 8;

    r = clamp(r, 0, 255);
    g = clamp(g, 0, 255);
    b = clamp(b, 0, 255);
    return (a << 24) | (uint(r) << 16) | (uint(g) << 8) | uint(b);
}

/* rr_texel for every datatype the interpreter decodes, which is all of
   PRIMARY_DATATYPE but 10, CI16 (SDK: Texture Mapping, p. 6-39 / PDF
   151); code 0, the guide's 2 bpp VQ, carries the S3TC block formats
   here, as the interpreter's r3d_texel reads it. Each fetch masks its
   address, then reads linear, like the interpreter; formats that fetch
   several times (RGB888, YUV 4:2:2) mask each fetch the way r3d_texel
   does, which gives the same address modulo the power-of-two mask for
   resident and staged levels alike.

   texel() stays one real SPIR-V function: the regen script marks it
   DontInline by name (texel, returning uint), so all its call sites
   share one body instead of an inlined copy each. The script stops
   with an error unless it finds exactly one such function, so a rename
   cannot slip through. */
uint
texel(uint dt, uint s3tc, uint palb, bool stgd, uint base, uint mask, uint lw,
      int u, int v)
{
    uint off = uint(v) * lw + uint(u);
    uint t;
    /* The host sets selector bit 4 when TEX_MAP_AEN is clear for an
       alpha-bearing format (Registers for CCE 3D Packets,
       SCALE_3D_CNTL). The mask is applied at decode, so filtering sees
       opaque fetched texels and a substituted border color keeps its
       own alpha. */
    uint amask = (dt & 0x10u) != 0u ? 0xff000000u : 0u;

    switch (dt) {
        case 1u: /* CI4: byte-aligned, index in the LOW nibble */
            t = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off));
            return pal[palb + (t & 0xfu)];
        case 2u: /* CI8: index into the 256-entry palette snapshot */
            t = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off));
            return pal[palb + t];
        case 19u:
        case 3u: /* ARGB1555; 1-bit alpha is a plain MSB test */
            t = tldu16(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off * 2u));
            return (((t & 0x8000u) != 0u) ? 0xff000000u : 0u)
                 | ((t & 0x7c00u) << 9) | ((t & 0x03e0u) << 6)
                 | ((t & 0x001fu) << 3) | amask;
        case 5u: { /* RGB888: 3 packed bytes per texel, LE B G R */
            uint a5 = taddr(stgd, base, mask, off * 3u);

            return 0xff000000u | (tldu8(stgd, a5 + 2u) << 16)
                 | (tldu8(stgd, a5 + 1u) << 8) | tldu8(stgd, a5);
        }
        case 7u: /* RGB332: shift-only channel expansion */
            t = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off));
            return 0xff000000u | ((t & 0xe0u) << 16) | ((t & 0x1cu) << 11)
                 | ((t & 0x03u) << 6);
        case 8u: /* Y8 gray scale: intensity to RGB, opaque */
            t = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off));
            return 0xff000000u | (t << 16) | (t << 8) | t;
        case 25u:
        case 9u: /* RGB8 gray scale: intensity in all four channels */
            t = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off));
            return (t << 24) | (t << 16) | (t << 8) | t | amask;
        /* 11 and 12 are the guide's "YUV 422 packed (VYUY)" and
           "(YVYU)" (RRG: DP_DATATYPE, p. 3-168 / PDF 186). The byte
           orders below are the interpreter's reading of those names;
           the guide gives no byte layout. */
        case 11u: /* bytes Y0 U Y1 V (YUY2 order), two texels per pair */
        case 12u: { /* bytes U Y0 V Y1 (UYVY order) */
            uint poff = (uint(v) * lw + uint(u & ~1)) * 2u;
            uint yb, cb, cr;

            if (dt == 11u) {
                yb = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, poff + uint(u & 1) * 2u));
                cb = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, poff + 1u));
                cr = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, poff + 3u));
            } else {
                yb = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, poff + 1u + uint(u & 1) * 2u));
                cb = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, poff));
                cr = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, poff + 2u));
            }
            return yuv_to_argb(0xffu, int(yb), int(cb), int(cr));
        }
        case 30u:
        case 14u: /* aYUV444 8:8:8:8: A[31:24] Y[23:16] U[15:8] V[7:0] */
            t = tldu32(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off * 4u));
            return yuv_to_argb(t >> 24, int((t >> 16) & 0xffu),
                               int((t >> 8) & 0xffu), int(t & 0xffu)) | amask;
        case 4u:
            t = tldu16(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off * 2u));
            return 0xff000000u | ((t & 0xf800u) << 8) | ((t & 0x07e0u) << 5)
                 | ((t & 0x001fu) << 3);
        case 31u:
        case 15u:
            t = tldu16(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off * 2u));
            return ((t & 0xf000u) << 16) | ((t & 0x0f00u) << 12)
                 | ((t & 0x00f0u) << 8) | ((t & 0x000fu) << 4) | amask;
        case 22u:
        case 6u:
            return tldu32(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, off * 4u)) | amask;
        case 16u:
        default: { /* 0: S3TC */
            uint bpitch = (lw + 3u) >> 2;
            uint boff   = (uint(v >> 2) * bpitch + uint(u >> 2))
                        * ((s3tc >= 2u) ? 16u : 8u);
            uint coff   = boff + ((s3tc >= 2u) ? 8u : 0u);
            uint texidx = (uint(v & 3) << 2) | uint(u & 3);
            uint a      = 0xffu;
            uint c0, c1, bits, sel, r0, g0, b0, r1, g1, b1;

            if (s3tc == 2u) {
                uint an = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, boff + (texidx >> 1)));

                a = ((texidx & 1u) != 0u) ? (an >> 4) : (an & 0xfu);
                a *= 0x11u;
            } else if (s3tc == 3u) {
                uint a0 = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, boff));
                uint a1 = tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, boff + 1u));
                uint bp = texidx * 3u;
                uint aw = tldu32(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask,
                                             boff + 2u + (bp >> 3)));
                uint ac = (aw >> (bp & 7u)) & 7u;

                if (ac == 0u)
                    a = a0;
                else if (ac == 1u)
                    a = a1;
                else if (a0 > a1)
                    a = ((8u - ac) * a0 + (ac - 1u) * a1) / 7u;
                else if (ac == 6u)
                    a = 0u;
                else if (ac == 7u)
                    a = 255u;
                else
                    a = ((6u - ac) * a0 + (ac - 1u) * a1) / 5u;
            }

            a |= amask >> 24;
            c0   = tldu16(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, coff));
            c1   = tldu16(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, coff + 2u));
            bits = tldu32(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, coff + 4u));
            sel  = (bits >> (texidx * 2u)) & 3u;
            r0 = ((c0 >> 11) & 0x1fu) * 255u / 31u;
            g0 = ((c0 >> 5) & 0x3fu) * 255u / 63u;
            b0 = (c0 & 0x1fu) * 255u / 31u;
            r1 = ((c1 >> 11) & 0x1fu) * 255u / 31u;
            g1 = ((c1 >> 5) & 0x3fu) * 255u / 63u;
            b1 = (c1 & 0x1fu) * 255u / 31u;
            switch (sel) {
                case 0u: return (a << 24) | (r0 << 16) | (g0 << 8) | b0;
                case 1u: return (a << 24) | (r1 << 16) | (g1 << 8) | b1;
                case 2u:
                    if (c0 > c1 || s3tc >= 2u)
                        return (a << 24) | (((2u * r0 + r1) / 3u) << 16)
                             | (((2u * g0 + g1) / 3u) << 8) | ((2u * b0 + b1) / 3u);
                    return (a << 24) | (((r0 + r1) >> 1) << 16)
                         | (((g0 + g1) >> 1) << 8) | ((b0 + b1) >> 1);
                default:
                    if (c0 > c1 || s3tc >= 2u)
                        return (a << 24) | (((r0 + 2u * r1) / 3u) << 16)
                             | (((g0 + 2u * g1) / 3u) << 8) | ((b0 + 2u * b1) / 3u);
                    return amask;
            }
        }
    }
}

/* rr_tex_level. The chroma key compares the nearest texel before
   filtering: want_near forces the nearest tap even under linear
   filtering and returns it in near_out, which every path assigns
   because it is a GLSL out parameter.

   The four bilinear taps are written out on purpose. A loop over one
   texel() call site gives a smaller module, but textured spans run
   slower in the gputri bench: with a runtime trip count the four
   fetches are not issued in parallel. */
uint
tex_level(int ti, uint stg, int slot, float s, float t, bool linear,
          bool want_near, out uint near_out)
{
    int  cslot = slot < 0 ? 0 : (slot > 10 ? 10 : slot);
    uint lw, lh, base, mask;
    bool stgd;
    uint dt   = FOLDF(stg, F_DT0, F_DT1, 0u);
    uint s3tc = FOLDF(stg, F_S3TC0, F_S3TC1, 1u);
    uint palb = tris[ti].pal_base;
    uint cls  = cfg(ti, stg, 2u);
    uint clt  = cfg(ti, stg, 3u);
    uint bord = cfg(ti, stg, 4u);

    slot_of(ti, stg, cslot, lw, lh, base, mask, stgd);

    near_out = 0u;
    if (!linear || want_near) {
        int  u = tex_wrap(sat_i32(floor(texcoord_fx(s * float(lw)))), int(lw), cls);
        int  v = tex_wrap(sat_i32(floor(texcoord_fx(t * float(lh)))), int(lh), clt);
        uint near = (u < 0 || v < 0) ? bord : texel(dt, s3tc, palb, stgd, base, mask, lw, u, v);

        /* CI formats key on the raw palette index, not the color */
        near_out = (want_near && u >= 0 && v >= 0 && (dt == 1u || dt == 2u))
            ? (tldu8(stgd, taddr(stgd, base, mask, uint(v) * lw + uint(u)))
               & (dt == 1u ? 0xfu : 0xffu))
            : near;
        if (!linear)
            return near;
    }

    {
        precise float fu = texcoord_fx(s * float(lw)) - 0.5;
        precise float fv = texcoord_fx(t * float(lh)) - 0.5;
        int           u0 = sat_i32(floor(fu));
        int           v0 = sat_i32(floor(fv));
        precise float wuf = (fu - float(u0)) * 256.0 + 0.5;
        precise float wvf = (fv - float(v0)) * 256.0 + 0.5;
        uint          wu = sat_u32(wuf);
        uint          wv = sat_u32(wvf);
        /* Clamp caps the raw successor at the upper edge before the
           increment; the other modes preserve their modulo addition. */
        int un = (cls & 3u) == 2u ? min(u0, int(lw) - 1) : u0;
        int vn = (clt & 3u) == 2u ? min(v0, int(lh) - 1) : v0;
        int uu0 = tex_wrap(u0, int(lw), cls), uu1 = tex_wrap(un + 1, int(lw), cls);
        int vv0 = tex_wrap(v0, int(lh), clt), vv1 = tex_wrap(vn + 1, int(lh), clt);
        uint c0 = (uu0 < 0 || vv0 < 0) ? bord : texel(dt, s3tc, palb, stgd, base, mask, lw, uu0, vv0);
        uint c1 = (uu1 < 0 || vv0 < 0) ? bord : texel(dt, s3tc, palb, stgd, base, mask, lw, uu1, vv0);
        uint c2 = (uu0 < 0 || vv1 < 0) ? bord : texel(dt, s3tc, palb, stgd, base, mask, lw, uu0, vv1);
        uint c3 = (uu1 < 0 || vv1 < 0) ? bord : texel(dt, s3tc, palb, stgd, base, mask, lw, uu1, vv1);

        return lerp_packed(lerp_packed(c0, c1, wu), lerp_packed(c2, c3, wu), wv);
    }
}

/* rr_tex_sample: the filter select, all at run time. want_near and
   near_out carry the chroma-key nearest tap: a mip-linear sample takes
   it from level A only, as the interpreter does, and every other path
   from the level it samples.

   The shape keeps the module small: every filter path first resolves
   its (slot, linear) pair and then reaches one shared tex_level call,
   with a second call only for the mip-linear B level. tex_level is
   large once inlined, so one call per filter path would multiply that
   size for each stage. */
uint
tex_sample(int ti, uint stg, float s, float t, float lod, bool has_lod,
           bool want_near, out uint near_out)
{
    uint minb   = cfg(ti, stg, 5u);
    uint mag    = cfg(ti, stg, 6u);
    uint mipdis = cfg(ti, stg, 7u);
    int  top    = stg == 0u ? tris[ti].top0 : tris[ti].top1;
    int  slotA  = top;
    int  slotB  = 0;
    bool linear = false;
    bool mipl   = false;
    uint wb     = 0u;

    if (!has_lod || mipdis != 0u || minb < 2u) {
        linear = has_lod ? (lod > 0.0 ? (minb & 1u) != 0u : (mag == 1u))
                         : !(minb == 0u && mag == 0u);
    } else if (lod <= 0.0) {
        linear = (mag == 1u);
    } else {
        precise float lvl = lod > float(top) ? float(top) : lod;

        linear = (minb == 3u || minb == 5u);
        mipl   = (minb == 4u || minb == 5u);
        if (mipl) {
            int           l0 = sat_i32(floor(lvl));
            precise float f  = lvl - float(l0);

            slotA = top - l0;
            slotB = top - (l0 + 1);
            if (slotB < 0)
                slotB = 0;
            wb = sat_u32(f * 256.0 + 0.5);
        } else
            slotA = top - sat_i32(lvl + 0.5);
    }

    {
        uint ca = tex_level(ti, stg, slotA, s, t, linear, want_near, near_out);
        uint nno, cb;

        if (!mipl)
            return ca;
        cb = tex_level(ti, stg, slotB, s, t, linear, false, nno);
        return lerp_packed(ca, cb, wb);
    }
}

/* rr_tex_combine, the interpreter's r3d_tex_combine: the full code
   book for the color and alpha halves. It takes the texel as floats.
   tex_combine unpacks a sampled texel; the texture-lighting pass
   (tex_light) passes the stage chain's output as its texel instead. */
void
tex_combine_sel(int ti, uint comb, uint fmsb, uint cfac, uint ifac,
                uint comba, uint afac, uint ifaca,
                precise inout float col[4], float tr, float tg, float tb,
                float ta, float int_color[4], bool first)
{
    precise float prev[4];
    precise float fc[3];
    precise float ci[3];
    precise float fa, ia;
    int           i;

    prev[0] = col[0]; prev[1] = col[1]; prev[2] = col[2]; prev[3] = col[3];

    switch (cfac) {
        case 0u: fc[0] = tris[ti].cc[0];       fc[1] = tris[ti].cc[1];       fc[2] = tris[ti].cc[2];       break;
        case 1u: fc[0] = 1.0 - tris[ti].cc[0]; fc[1] = 1.0 - tris[ti].cc[1]; fc[2] = 1.0 - tris[ti].cc[2]; break;
        case 5u: fc[0] = 1.0 - tr;             fc[1] = 1.0 - tg;             fc[2] = 1.0 - tb;             break;
        case 6u: fc[0] = fc[1] = fc[2] = ta;       break;
        case 7u: fc[0] = fc[1] = fc[2] = 1.0 - ta; break;
        case 8u: fc[0] = prev[0]; fc[1] = prev[1]; fc[2] = prev[2]; break;
        default: fc[0] = tr; fc[1] = tg; fc[2] = tb; break;
    }
    switch (ifac) {
        case 2u: ci[0] = tris[ti].cc[0]; ci[1] = tris[ti].cc[1]; ci[2] = tris[ti].cc[2]; break;
        case 3u: ci[0] = ci[1] = ci[2] = tris[ti].cc[3]; break;
        case 5u: ci[0] = ci[1] = ci[2] = int_color[3]; break;
        case 8u: ci[0] = prev[0]; ci[1] = prev[1]; ci[2] = prev[2]; break;
        case 9u: ci[0] = ci[1] = ci[2] = prev[3]; break;
        default: ci[0] = int_color[0]; ci[1] = int_color[1]; ci[2] = int_color[2]; break;
    }

    switch (comb) {
        case 0u:
            if (fmsb != 0u) {
                for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = fc[i] - ci[i]; if (col[i] < 0.0) col[i] = 0.0; }
                break;
            }
            col[0] = tr; col[1] = tg; col[2] = tb;
            break;
        case 1u:
            col[0] = fc[0]; col[1] = fc[1]; col[2] = fc[2];
            break;
        case 2u:
            col[0] = ci[0]; col[1] = ci[1]; col[2] = ci[2];
            break;
        case 4u:
            if (fmsb != 0u) {
                col[0] = ci[0] * (1.0 - tr) + fc[0] * tr;
                col[1] = ci[1] * (1.0 - tg) + fc[1] * tg;
                col[2] = ci[2] * (1.0 - tb) + fc[2] * tb;
            } else {
                for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = ci[i] * fc[i] * 2.0; if (col[i] > 1.0) col[i] = 1.0; }
            }
            break;
        case 5u:
            if (fmsb != 0u) {
                precise float t5[3] = float[3](tr, tg, tb);

                for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = fc[i] + ci[i] * (1.0 - t5[i]); if (col[i] > 1.0) col[i] = 1.0; }
            } else {
                for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = ci[i] * fc[i] * 4.0; if (col[i] > 1.0) col[i] = 1.0; }
            }
            break;
        case 6u:
            if (fmsb != 0u) {
                precise float t6[3] = float[3](tr, tg, tb);

                for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = fc[i] + ci[i] * t6[i]; if (col[i] > 1.0) col[i] = 1.0; }
            } else {
                for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = ci[i] + fc[i]; if (col[i] > 1.0) col[i] = 1.0; }
            }
            break;
        case 7u:
            for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = ci[i] + fc[i] - 0.5; col[i] = col[i] < 0.0 ? 0.0 : (col[i] > 1.0 ? 1.0 : col[i]); }
            break;
        case 8u: {
            precise float f8 = int_color[3];

            for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) col[i] = ci[i] * (1.0 - f8) + fc[i] * f8;
            break;
        }
        case 9u:
            for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) col[i] = ci[i] * (1.0 - ta) + fc[i] * ta;
            break;
        case 10u:
            for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) col[i] = ci[i] * (1.0 - tris[ti].cc[3]) + fc[i] * tris[ti].cc[3];
            break;
        case 11u:
            for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = fc[i] + ci[i] * (1.0 - ta); if (col[i] > 1.0) col[i] = 1.0; }
            break;
        case 12u:
            for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) col[i] = ci[i] * (1.0 - prev[3]) + fc[i] * prev[3];
            break;
        case 13u:
            for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = fc[i] + ci[i] * ta; if (col[i] > 1.0) col[i] = 1.0; }
            break;
        case 14u:
            for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { col[i] = (ci[i] + fc[i] - 0.5) * 2.0; col[i] = col[i] < 0.0 ? 0.0 : (col[i] > 1.0 ? 1.0 : col[i]); }
            break;
        case 15u:
            for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) col[i] = ci[i] * (1.0 - tris[ti].cc[i]) + fc[i] * tris[ti].cc[i];
            break;
        default:
            col[0] = ci[0] * fc[0]; col[1] = ci[1] * fc[1]; col[2] = ci[2] * fc[2];
            break;
    }

    fa = (afac == 7u) ? (1.0 - ta) : ta;
    switch (ifaca) {
        case 1u:  ia = tris[ti].cc[3]; break;
        case 2u:  ia = int_color[3];   break;
        default:  ia = prev[3];        break;
    }
    switch (comba) {
        case 0u: /* the texel alpha on the first stage, the incoming
                    alpha after it, as in the interpreter */
            col[3] = first ? ta : prev[3];
            break;
        case 1u:
            col[3] = fa;
            break;
        case 2u:
            col[3] = ia;
            break;
        case 4u:
            col[3] = ia * fa * 2.0; if (col[3] > 1.0) col[3] = 1.0;
            break;
        case 6u:
            col[3] = ia + fa; if (col[3] > 1.0) col[3] = 1.0;
            break;
        case 7u:
            col[3] = ia + fa - 0.5; col[3] = col[3] < 0.0 ? 0.0 : (col[3] > 1.0 ? 1.0 : col[3]);
            break;
        case 5u:
            col[3] = ia * fa * 4.0; if (col[3] > 1.0) col[3] = 1.0;
            break;
        case 14u:
            col[3] = (ia + fa - 0.5) * 2.0; col[3] = col[3] < 0.0 ? 0.0 : (col[3] > 1.0 ? 1.0 : col[3]);
            break;
        default:
            col[3] = ia * fa;
            break;
    }
}

/* one texture stage: the folded (or per-triangle) selectors applied to
   the packed texel */
void
tex_combine(int ti, uint stg, precise inout float col[4], uint tx,
            float int_color[4])
{
    tex_combine_sel(ti,
                    FOLDC(stg, 0u, C_COMB0, C_COMB1),
                    FOLDC(stg, 1u, C_FMSB0, C_FMSB1),
                    FOLDC(stg, 2u, C_CFAC0, C_CFAC1),
                    FOLDC(stg, 3u, C_IFAC0, C_IFAC1),
                    FOLDC(stg, 4u, C_COMBA0, C_COMBA1),
                    FOLDC(stg, 5u, C_AFAC0, C_AFAC1),
                    FOLDC(stg, 6u, C_IFACA0, C_IFACA1),
                    col, div255((tx >> 16) & 0xffu), div255((tx >> 8) & 0xffu),
                    div255(tx & 0xffu), div255(tx >> 24), int_color, stg == 0u);
}

/* Texture lighting. The guide makes the first argument the output of
   the texture combine units and the second the interpolated color or
   alpha (SDK: Texture Mapping, p. 6-42 / PDF 154). So the chain output
   is the texel here, and the factors are fixed, in the codes of the
   stage combine fields: COLOR_FACTOR 4, texel color, and INPUT_FACTOR
   4, interpolated color (SDK: Texture Mapping, p. 6-43 / PDF 155);
   ALPHA_FACTOR 6, texel alpha, and INPUT_FACTOR_ALPHA 2, interpolated
   alpha (SDK: Texture Mapping, p. 6-44 / PDF 156). The light word
   holds the TEX_LIGHT_FN color function in [3:0], the subtract bit in
   [4] and the ALPHA_LIGHT_FN alpha function in [7:5]. */
void
tex_light(int ti, uint lw, precise inout float col[4], float int_color[4])
{
    precise float cr = col[0], cg = col[1], cb = col[2], ca = col[3];

    tex_combine_sel(ti, lw & 0xfu, (lw >> 4) & 1u, 4u, 4u, (lw >> 5) & 7u,
                    6u, 2u, col, cr, cg, cb, ca, int_color, false);
}

/* ---- destination surface: r3d_dst_read / r3d_dst_write. daddr is a BYTE
   address; the 16bpp formats (3 ARGB1555, 4 RGB565, 15 ARGB4444) index the
   u16 view, ARGB8888 (6) the word view. ---- */

uint
dst_read(uint daddr, uint dt, bool dstg)
{
    uint v;

    if (dt == 6u) {
        if (!dstg && (daddr & 3u) != 0u)
            return ldu32(daddr);
        return cw_ld(dstg, daddr >> 2);
    }
    if (!dstg && (daddr & 1u) != 0u)
        v = ldu16(daddr);
    else
        v = c16_ld(dstg, daddr >> 1);

    switch (dt) {
        case 3u:
            return ((v & 0x8000u) != 0u ? 0xff000000u : 0u)
                 | ((v & 0x7c00u) << 9) | ((v & 0x03e0u) << 6)
                 | ((v & 0x001fu) << 3);
        case 15u:
            return ((v & 0xf000u) << 16) | ((v & 0x0f00u) << 12)
                 | ((v & 0x00f0u) << 8) | ((v & 0x000fu) << 4);
        default: /* 4 */
            return 0xff000000u | ((v & 0xf800u) << 8) | ((v & 0x07e0u) << 5)
                 | ((v & 0x001fu) << 3);
    }
}

/* 8->n-bit truncation dither offset on one channel, saturating */
uint
dq(uint v8, uint add)
{
    return min(v8 + add, 255u);
}

/* bay < 0 = dithering off. pmask is PLANE_3D_MASK_C (RRG:
   PLANE_3D_MASK_C, p. 3-260 / PDF 278), a bitmask on the color planes;
   the interpreter applies it to the packed pixel after dithering, not
   to the ARGB value. */
void
dst_write(uint daddr, uint dt, uint argb, int bay, uint pmask, bool dstg)
{
    uint a = (argb >> 24) & 0xffu;
    uint r = (argb >> 16) & 0xffu;
    uint g = (argb >> 8) & 0xffu;
    uint b = argb & 0xffu;
    uint raw, m, dcell;

    /* 8888 has no quantization, so nothing to dither */
    if (dt == 6u) {
        if (pmask != 0xffffffffu)
            argb = (argb & pmask) | (dst_read(daddr, dt, dstg) & ~pmask);
        if (!dstg && (daddr & 3u) != 0u)
            ust32(daddr, argb);
        else
            cw_st(dstg, daddr >> 2, argb);
        return;
    }
    dcell = daddr >> 1;

    switch (dt) {
        case 3u: /* ARGB1555; 1-bit alpha stays a plain MSB test */
            if (bay >= 0) {
                r = dq(r, uint(bay) >> 1);
                g = dq(g, uint(bay) >> 1);
                b = dq(b, uint(bay) >> 1);
            }
            raw = ((a << 8) & 0x8000u) | ((r << 7) & 0x7c00u)
                | ((g << 2) & 0x03e0u) | (b >> 3);
            break;
        case 15u: /* ARGB4444 */
            if (bay >= 0) {
                a = dq(a, uint(bay));
                r = dq(r, uint(bay));
                g = dq(g, uint(bay));
                b = dq(b, uint(bay));
            }
            raw = ((a << 8) & 0xf000u) | ((r << 4) & 0x0f00u)
                | (g & 0x00f0u) | (b >> 4);
            break;
        default: /* 4 = RGB565 */
            if (bay >= 0) {
                r = dq(r, uint(bay) >> 1);
                g = dq(g, uint(bay) >> 2);
                b = dq(b, uint(bay) >> 1);
            }
            raw = ((r << 8) & 0xf800u) | ((g << 3) & 0x07e0u) | (b >> 3);
            break;
    }
    m = pmask & 0xffffu;
    if (!dstg && (daddr & 1u) != 0u) {
        if (m != 0xffffu)
            raw = (raw & m) | (ldu16(daddr) & ~m);
        ust16(daddr, raw);
    } else {
        if (m != 0xffffu)
            raw = (raw & m) | (c16_ld(dstg, dcell) & ~m);
        c16_st(dstg, dcell, raw);
    }
}

/* ref_aux_pass on the per-triangle copies of the auxiliary scissor
   rectangles; aux_cntl is 0 when the draw has them off */
bool
aux_pass(int ti, int x, int y)
{
    bool have_add = false, in_add = false;

    if (FOLDB(FB_ZAUX, tris[ti].aux_cntl, 0u) == 0u)
        return true;
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        if ((tris[ti].aux_cntl & (1u << (i * 2))) == 0u)
            continue;
        bool inr = x >= tris[ti].aux_x0[i] && x <= tris[ti].aux_x1[i]
                && y >= tris[ti].aux_y0[i] && y <= tris[ti].aux_y1[i];

        if ((tris[ti].aux_cntl & (2u << (i * 2))) != 0u) {
            if (inr)
                return false;
        } else {
            have_add = true;
            if (inr)
                in_add = true;
        }
    }
    return have_add ? in_add : true;
}

/* ref_blend_factor */
void
blend_factor(uint code, float sc[4], float dc[4], precise out float o[4])
{
    precise float f;

    switch (code & 0xfu) {
        case 0x0u: o[0] = o[1] = o[2] = o[3] = 0.0; return;
        case 0x1u: o[0] = o[1] = o[2] = o[3] = 1.0; return;
        case 0x2u: o[0] = sc[0]; o[1] = sc[1]; o[2] = sc[2]; o[3] = sc[3]; return;
        case 0x3u: o[0] = 1.0 - sc[0]; o[1] = 1.0 - sc[1]; o[2] = 1.0 - sc[2]; o[3] = 1.0 - sc[3]; return;
        case 0x4u: o[0] = o[1] = o[2] = o[3] = sc[3]; return;
        case 0x5u: o[0] = o[1] = o[2] = o[3] = 1.0 - sc[3]; return;
        case 0x6u: o[0] = o[1] = o[2] = o[3] = dc[3]; return;
        case 0x7u: o[0] = o[1] = o[2] = o[3] = 1.0 - dc[3]; return;
        case 0x8u: o[0] = dc[0]; o[1] = dc[1]; o[2] = dc[2]; o[3] = dc[3]; return;
        case 0x9u: o[0] = 1.0 - dc[0]; o[1] = 1.0 - dc[1]; o[2] = 1.0 - dc[2]; o[3] = 1.0 - dc[3]; return;
        case 0xau:
            f = sc[3] < 1.0 - dc[3] ? sc[3] : 1.0 - dc[3];
            o[0] = o[1] = o[2] = f;
            o[3] = 1.0;
            return;
        default:
            o[0] = o[1] = o[2] = o[3] = 1.0;
            return;
    }
}

int64_t
edge_i64(uvec2 e)
{
    return int64_t(uint64_t(e.x) | (uint64_t(e.y) << 32));
}

void
pixel(int si, int ti, uint i)
{
    int  px = spans[si].x0 + int(i);
    /* ladder slot: table-fog draws have two words per pixel (depth,
       then the 32-bit fog fraction), every other draw one */
    uint lat = spans[si].px_base + i * ((tris[ti].ftab_en != 0u) ? 2u : 1u);
    bool cdead = (C_FOLD != 0)
        ? (C_DEAD != 0)
        : (tris[ti].wmask == 0u
           && FOLDB(FB_ZAUX, tris[ti].atest_en, 0u) == 0u
           && tris[ti].ck_ctl == 0u);
    precise float ef0 = 0.0, ef1 = 0.0, ef2 = 0.0;

    /* The host clipped the span to the three edge inequalities, so every
       pixel in it is covered and the edges are not tested here. A draw
       with dead color needs no edge values; one with live color uses
       them only for the barycentric weights. The capture sets pad1 when
       all three edge values stay in signed 32-bit range across the span,
       and the 32-bit path is then exact; otherwise the 64-bit path
       runs. */
    if (!cdead) {
        if ((B_FOLD & FB_EDGE) != 0 || spans[si].pad1 != 0u) {
            int e0 = int(spans[si].e0.x) + int(i) * int(tris[ti].e0dxi.x);
            int e1 = int(spans[si].e1.x) + int(i) * int(tris[ti].e1dxi.x);
            int e2 = int(spans[si].e2.x) + int(i) * int(tris[ti].e2dxi.x);

            ef0 = float(e0);
            ef1 = float(e1);
            ef2 = float(e2);
        } else {
            int64_t e0 = edge_i64(spans[si].e0)
                       + int64_t(i) * edge_i64(tris[ti].e0dxi);
            int64_t e1 = edge_i64(spans[si].e1)
                       + int64_t(i) * edge_i64(tris[ti].e1dxi);
            int64_t e2 = edge_i64(spans[si].e2)
                       + int64_t(i) * edge_i64(tris[ti].e2dxi);

            ef0 = float(e0);
            ef1 = float(e1);
            ef2 = float(e2);
        }
    }
    if (!aux_pass(ti, px, spans[si].py))
        return;
    /* A clear modeled stipple bit discards before depth and stencil;
       destination coordinates select the captured row and reversed bit. */
    if (tris[ti].stip_en != 0u
        && ((tris[ti].stipple[spans[si].py & 31] >> (31 - (px & 31))) & 1u) == 0u)
        return;

    /* Barycentric weights only (three multiplies). The four Gouraud
       channels wait until after the Z and stencil tests, so a rejected
       pixel does not build a color it discards. Dead color needs
       neither. */
    precise float w0 = 0.0, w1 = 0.0, w2 = 0.0;

    if (!cdead) {
        w0 = ef0 * tris[ti].invs;
        w1 = ef1 * tris[ti].invs;
        w2 = ef2 * tris[ti].invs;
    }

    /* ---- Z and stencil tests (runtime selectors; depth from the
       pre-pass) ---- zbpp 2 reads the u16 cell whole; zbpp 4 reads the
       word and shifts the depth field out from beside the stencil byte.
       zcell is the resolved byte address, so the writebacks below hit
       the same cell. With stencil on, a failed test cannot discard yet:
       the interpreter runs the stencil update after the alpha test, so
       the results wait in zres and sres and the discard comes later. A
       draw with stencil on and the Z test off arrives with zfn set to
       7 (always) and z_wr 0 by the capture side. */
    uint zi = 0u, zcell = 0u, zbuf = 0u;
    uint zbpp = tris[ti].zbpp, zshift = tris[ti].zshift;
    uint zmax = (zbpp == 4u) ? 0xffffffu : 0xffffu;
    bool zres = true, sres = true, sten = false, zstg = false, zua = false;
    uint sbuf = 0u, sshift = 0u;

    if (V_Z != 0) {
        uint zrw = spans[si].zrow;

        /* bits [31:24] hold the table-fog entry (ftab_en draws only,
           which read it again below); depth uses at most the low 24 */
        zi    = lad[lat] & zmax;
        zstg  = (zrw & 0x80000000u) != 0u;
        zcell = zstg ? ((zrw & 0x7fffffffu) + uint(px) * zbpp)
                     : ((zrw + uint(px) * zbpp) & pc.vram_mask);
        /* straddling resident cell (Z_OFFSET not a multiple of the cell
           size): reads assemble bytes, writes below are byte-lane
           atomic */
        zua   = !zstg && (zcell & (zbpp - 1u)) != 0u;
        zbuf  = (zbpp == 4u)
                    ? (((zua ? ldu32(zcell) : zw_ld(zstg, zcell >> 2))
                        >> zshift) & zmax)
                    : (zua ? ldu16(zcell) : z16_ld(zstg, zcell >> 1));
        zres  = cmp_fn(FOLDB(FB_ZAUX, tris[ti].zfn, 2u), zi, zbuf) != 0;
        sten  = (C_FOLD != 0)
            ? (S_STEN != 0)
            : (FOLDB(FB_ZAUX, tris[ti].sten_ctl & 1u, 0u) != 0u);
        if (sten) {
            /* stencil sits in the byte the depth field leaves free: the top
           byte when depth is the low 24 bits (zshift 0), the layout the
           guide gives (SDK: Setting 3D Render States, p. 6-56 / PDF
           168), else the low byte */
            sshift = (zshift != 0u) ? 0u : 24u;
            sbuf   = ((zua ? ldu32(zcell) : zw_ld(zstg, zcell >> 2))
                      >> sshift) & 0xffu;
            {
                uint svm = (tris[ti].sten_rm >> 8) & 0xffu;

                sres = cmp_fn((tris[ti].sten_ctl >> 4) & 7u,
                              tris[ti].sten_rm & svm, sbuf & svm) != 0;
            }
        } else if (!zres)
            return;
    }

    /* Gouraud color, after the Z and stencil tests: with stencil off, a
       Z-rejected pixel has already returned above. col is only read
       when the color is live (!cdead). */
    precise float col[4];

    if (!cdead) {
        col[0] = w0 * tris[ti].vca[0] + w1 * tris[ti].vcb[0] + w2 * tris[ti].vcc[0];
        col[1] = w0 * tris[ti].vca[1] + w1 * tris[ti].vcb[1] + w2 * tris[ti].vcc[1];
        col[2] = w0 * tris[ti].vca[2] + w1 * tris[ti].vcb[2] + w2 * tris[ti].vcc[2];
        col[3] = w0 * tris[ti].vca[3] + w1 * tris[ti].vcb[3] + w2 * tris[ti].vcc[3];
    }

    if (V_TEX >= 1 && !cdead) {
        precise float int_color[4];

        int_color[0] = col[0]; int_color[1] = col[1];
        int_color[2] = col[2]; int_color[3] = col[3];

        /* Perspective divide, a runtime per-triangle selector. An affine
           draw (PRIM_TEX_CNTL_C bit 14, the Mesa r128 macro
           R128_TEX_PERSPECTIVE_DISABLE) never forms rhw and leaves ir at
           1.0, so S and T are the plain barycentric interpolation. This
           is the interpreter's !do_persp path, which also skips the
           rhw dot product. */
        bool          persp = (tris[ti].persp & 1u) != 0u;
        precise float rhw = persp ? (w0 * tris[ti].arhw + w1 * tris[ti].brhw
                                     + w2 * tris[ti].crhw)
                                  : 0.0;
        precise float ir  = 1.0;

        if (persp && rhw != 0.0)
            ir = fdiv_cr(1.0, rhw);

        /* A secondary-only draw keeps the iterated color for stage 1;
           bit 1 skips stage 0's sampler, chroma keys, and combine. */
        if ((tris[ti].persp & 2u) == 0u) {
            precise float sp = w0 * tris[ti].sta + w1 * tris[ti].stb + w2 * tris[ti].stc;
            precise float tp = w0 * tris[ti].tta + w1 * tris[ti].ttb + w2 * tris[ti].ttc;
            precise float s  = sp * ir;
            precise float t  = tp * ir;
            precise float lod = 0.0;

            if (V_LOD != 0) {
                precise float dsx, dsy, dtx, dty;

                if (persp) {
                    precise float wp  = rhw;
                    precise float iw2 = (wp != 0.0) ? fdiv_cr(1.0, wp * wp) : 0.0;

                    dsx = (tris[ti].dSdx * wp - sp * tris[ti].dWdx) * iw2;
                    dsy = (tris[ti].dSdy * wp - sp * tris[ti].dWdy) * iw2;
                    dtx = (tris[ti].dTdx * wp - tp * tris[ti].dWdx) * iw2;
                    dty = (tris[ti].dTdy * wp - tp * tris[ti].dWdy) * iw2;
                } else {
                    dsx = tris[ti].dSdx; dsy = tris[ti].dSdy;
                    dtx = tris[ti].dTdx; dty = tris[ti].dTdy;
                }

                dsx *= tris[ti].texw0;
                dsy *= tris[ti].texw0;
                dtx *= tris[ti].texh0;
                dty *= tris[ti].texh0;
                {
                    precise float ax2  = dsx * dsx + dtx * dtx;
                    precise float ay2  = dsy * dsy + dty * dty;
                    precise float rho2 = ax2 > ay2 ? ax2 : ay2;

                    lod = rho2 > 0.0 ? 0.5 * log2f_fast(rho2) + tris[ti].lod_bias
                                     : -1000.0;
                }
            }
            {
                uint tnear = 0u;
                uint tx = tex_sample(ti, 0u, s, t, lod, V_LOD != 0,
                                     tris[ti].ck_ctl != 0u, tnear);

                /* Chroma key (stage 0 only), in interpreter order: it
                   compares the nearest texel before filtering and
                   discards before the combine, the alpha test and the
                   stencil update, so a keyed-out pixel changes nothing.
                   Bit 0 of ck_ctl enables the CLR_CMP_FCN_3D key, where
                   bit 2 set rejects on equal (function 3) and clear on
                   not equal (function 2); bit 1 enables the texture
                   color-compare key, which rejects on equal. */
                if ((tris[ti].ck_ctl & 1u) != 0u) {
                    bool eq = (tnear & tris[ti].ck3d_msk)
                           == (tris[ti].ck3d_clr & tris[ti].ck3d_msk);

                    if (((tris[ti].ck_ctl & 4u) != 0u) ? eq : !eq)
                        return;
                }
                if ((tris[ti].ck_ctl & 2u) != 0u
                    && (tnear & tris[ti].ckc_msk)
                       == (tris[ti].ckc_clr & tris[ti].ckc_msk))
                    return;
                tex_combine(ti, 0u, col, tx, int_color);
            }
        }

        /* ---- stage 1 (sec_sel and need_lod2 read at run time) ---- */
        if (V_TEX == 2) {
            bool          sel  = FOLDB(FB_ZAUX, tris[ti].sec_sel, 0u) != 0u;
            /* The stage's own perspective enable and W, runtime
               per-triangle selectors like persp. When they match the
               primary stage's, rhw and ir are the same values and are
               reused; otherwise the stage forms its own W dot product
               and reciprocal (1.0 with perspective off), the
               interpreter's rhw2 and ir2. */
            bool          persp2 = tris[ti].persp2 != 0u;
            bool          selw   = tris[ti].sel_w != 0u;
            bool          own    = selw || persp2 != persp;
            precise float rhw2   = rhw;
            precise float ir2    = ir;
            precise float sp2  = sel ? (w0 * tris[ti].s2a + w1 * tris[ti].s2b + w2 * tris[ti].s2c)
                                     : (w0 * tris[ti].sta + w1 * tris[ti].stb + w2 * tris[ti].stc);
            precise float tp2  = sel ? (w0 * tris[ti].t2a + w1 * tris[ti].t2b + w2 * tris[ti].t2c)
                                     : (w0 * tris[ti].tta + w1 * tris[ti].ttb + w2 * tris[ti].ttc);
            precise float s2, t2;
            precise float lod2 = 0.0;
            bool          hl2  = FOLDB(FB_ZAUX, tris[ti].need_lod2, 1u) != 0u;

            if (own) {
                rhw2 = 0.0;
                ir2  = 1.0;
                if (persp2) {
                    rhw2 = w0 * tris[ti].a2rhw + w1 * tris[ti].b2rhw + w2 * tris[ti].c2rhw;
                    if (rhw2 != 0.0)
                        ir2 = fdiv_cr(1.0, rhw2);
                }
            }
            s2 = sp2 * ir2;
            t2 = tp2 * ir2;

            if (hl2) {
                precise float gsx = sel ? tris[ti].dS2dx : tris[ti].dSdx;
                precise float gsy = sel ? tris[ti].dS2dy : tris[ti].dSdy;
                precise float gtx = sel ? tris[ti].dT2dx : tris[ti].dTdx;
                precise float gty = sel ? tris[ti].dT2dy : tris[ti].dTdy;
                precise float dsx, dsy, dtx, dty;

                if (persp2) {
                    precise float wp  = rhw2;
                    precise float iw2 = (wp != 0.0) ? fdiv_cr(1.0, wp * wp) : 0.0;
                    precise float gwx = selw ? tris[ti].dW2dx : tris[ti].dWdx;
                    precise float gwy = selw ? tris[ti].dW2dy : tris[ti].dWdy;

                    dsx = (gsx * wp - sp2 * gwx) * iw2;
                    dsy = (gsy * wp - sp2 * gwy) * iw2;
                    dtx = (gtx * wp - tp2 * gwx) * iw2;
                    dty = (gty * wp - tp2 * gwy) * iw2;
                } else {
                    dsx = gsx; dsy = gsy; dtx = gtx; dty = gty;
                }

                dsx *= tris[ti].texw1;
                dsy *= tris[ti].texw1;
                dtx *= tris[ti].texh1;
                dty *= tris[ti].texh1;
                {
                    precise float ax2  = dsx * dsx + dtx * dtx;
                    precise float ay2  = dsy * dsy + dty * dty;
                    precise float rho2 = ax2 > ay2 ? ax2 : ay2;

                    lod2 = rho2 > 0.0 ? 0.5 * log2f_fast(rho2) + tris[ti].lod_bias
                                      : -1000.0;
                }
            }
            {
                uint nn2;
                uint tx2 = tex_sample(ti, 1u, s2, t2, lod2, hl2, false, nn2);

                tex_combine(ti, 1u, col, tx2, int_color);
            }
        }
        /* texture lighting after the stage chain (rage128_texstage_run) */
        {
            uint lw = FOLDL();

            if (lw != 0u)
                tex_light(ti, lw, col, int_color);
        }
    }

    /* Separate specular add (TEX_CNTL_C bit 11, the Mesa r128 macro
       R128_SPEC_LIGHT_ENABLE), in interpreter order: after the texture
       stages, before fog and the alpha test. RGB only, saturating;
       alpha is unchanged. */
    if (!cdead && tris[ti].spec_en != 0u) {
        col[0] = col[0] + (w0 * tris[ti].spa[0] + w1 * tris[ti].spb[0] + w2 * tris[ti].spc[0]);
        col[1] = col[1] + (w0 * tris[ti].spa[1] + w1 * tris[ti].spb[1] + w2 * tris[ti].spc[1]);
        col[2] = col[2] + (w0 * tris[ti].spa[2] + w1 * tris[ti].spb[2] + w2 * tris[ti].spc[2]);
        if (col[0] > 1.0) col[0] = 1.0;
        if (col[1] > 1.0) col[1] = 1.0;
        if (col[2] > 1.0) col[2] = 1.0;
    }

    /* Fog, in interpreter order: after the specular add, before the
       alpha test. Final color = f * C + (1 - f) * fog color, on RGB
       (SDK: Setting 3D Render States, p. 6-51 / PDF 163). Vertex fog
       interpolates the per-vertex factor and clamps it, since it can
       leave [0, 1] near the edges. Table fog, as the interpreter models
       it, blends the table entry the pre-pass chose with the next one
       by the 32-bit fraction, in the interpreter's float operations:
       t = float(fr) * 2^-32, f = (T[i] + (T[i1] - T[i]) * t) / 255,
       with the divide correctly rounded. */
    if (!cdead && tris[ti].fog_en != 0u) {
        precise float f;

        if (tris[ti].ftab_en != 0u) {
            /* The ladder supplies the fog index and fraction even
               without a live depth cell. Read them here so the texture
               stages do not keep the operands live, as with Z writeback. */
            uint fi = lad[lat] >> 24;
            uint fr = lad[lat + 1u];
            uint i1 = min(fi + 1u, 255u);
            precise float t  = float(fr) * (1.0 / 4294967296.0);
            precise float fa = float((tris[ti].fog_table[fi >> 2] >> ((fi & 3u) * 8u)) & 0xffu);
            precise float fd = float(int((tris[ti].fog_table[i1 >> 2] >> ((i1 & 3u) * 8u)) & 0xffu)
                                     - int((tris[ti].fog_table[fi >> 2] >> ((fi & 3u) * 8u)) & 0xffu));
            precise float fn = fa + fd * t;

            f = fdiv_cr(fn, 255.0);
        } else {
            f = w0 * tris[ti].fga + w1 * tris[ti].fgb + w2 * tris[ti].fgc;
            if (f < 0.0) f = 0.0;
            if (f > 1.0) f = 1.0;
        }
        col[0] = col[0] * f + tris[ti].fogr * (1.0 - f);
        col[1] = col[1] * f + tris[ti].fogg * (1.0 - f);
        col[2] = col[2] * f + tris[ti].fogb * (1.0 - f);
    }

    /* Alpha test, in interpreter order: after the texture stages,
       before the stencil update, the color read and the Z writeback. A
       pixel that fails it changes none of them, while a pixel that
       failed the stencil or Z test still runs the stencil update
       below. */
    if (FOLDB(FB_ZAUX, tris[ti].atest_en, 0u) != 0u
        && cmp_fn(tris[ti].atest_fn, sat_u32(col[3] * 255.0 + 0.5),
                  tris[ti].atest_ref) == 0)
        return;

    /* Stencil update, then the deferred discard. The op is picked by
       the test outcome (sfail, zfail or zpass) and applied to the
       stencil byte under the write mask; it runs even when a test
       failed. Ops 0 to 5 are the guide's table (SDK: Setting 3D Render
       States, p. 6-57 / PDF 169). Ops 6 and 7, increment and decrement
       with wrap, are not in that table: they follow Mesa r128, whose
       R128_STENCIL_S_FAIL_INC_WRAP and R128_STENCIL_S_FAIL_DEC_WRAP
       are marked as guesses there. The Z writeback below keeps this
       byte. An aligned cell belongs to this pixel alone, so a plain
       read-modify-write is safe; a straddling cell and the commutative
       class below need atomics. */
    if (V_Z != 0 && sten) {
        uint sop  = !sres ? ((tris[ti].sten_ctl >> 8) & 7u)
                          : (zres ? ((tris[ti].sten_ctl >> 12) & 7u)
                                  : ((tris[ti].sten_ctl >> 16) & 7u));
        uint swm  = (tris[ti].sten_rm >> 16) & 0xffu;
        /* address the stencil byte, then the word holding it: for an
           aligned cell this is w = zcell >> 2 and ssw = sshift; for a
           straddling cell the byte can be in either word */
        uint sba  = zcell + (sshift >> 3);
        uint w    = sba >> 2;
        uint ssw  = (sba & 3u) * 8u;

        /* Commutative-class draw. This test must match the host's
           class in capture_span_class (sctl_of), which the batch
           builder uses: it may pack overlapping spans of the same class
           into one pass. The class is dead color, no Z write, stencil
           compare 7 (always, so the sfail op never runs) and zpass and
           zfail ops in 0 to 4 (keep, zero, replace, saturating
           increment, saturating decrement). Any two updates
           in the class commute, so applying them through a
           compare-and-swap loop in any order gives the result of the
           serial order. The loop never changes the depth bits, so the
           Z test read above stays valid. */
        uint zp_op = (tris[ti].sten_ctl >> 12) & 7u;
        uint zf_op = (tris[ti].sten_ctl >> 16) & 7u;
        /* the zpass and zfail ops must also commute with each other
           (equal, or one of them keep): overlapping spans hit the same
           pixel with different Z results, so the pixel sees a mix of
           the two ops in any order */
        bool s_atomic = cdead && tris[ti].z_wr == 0u
                        && ((tris[ti].sten_ctl >> 4) & 7u) == 7u
                        && zp_op <= 4u && zf_op <= 4u
                        && (zp_op == zf_op || zp_op == 0u || zf_op == 0u);

        if (s_atomic && sop != 0u) {
            for (;;) {
                uint cur = zw_ld(zstg, w);
                uint sb  = (cur >> ssw) & 0xffu;
                uint sn  = sb;

                switch (sop) {
                    case 1u: sn = 0u;                            break;
                    case 2u: sn = tris[ti].sten_rm & 0xffu;      break;
                    case 3u: sn = sb == 0xffu ? 0xffu : sb + 1u; break;
                    default: sn = sb == 0u ? 0u : sb - 1u;       break;
                }
                sn = (sb & ~swm) | (sn & swm);
                if (sn == sb)
                    break;
                if (zw_cas(zstg, w, cur,
                           (cur & ~(0xffu << ssw))
                               | (sn << ssw)) == cur)
                    break;
            }
        } else if (!s_atomic) {
            uint snew = sbuf;

            switch (sop) {
                case 0u:                                           break; /* KEEP     */
                case 1u: snew = 0u;                                break; /* ZERO     */
                case 2u: snew = tris[ti].sten_rm & 0xffu;          break; /* REPLACE  */
                case 3u: snew = sbuf == 0xffu ? 0xffu : sbuf + 1u; break; /* INC sat  */
                case 4u: snew = sbuf == 0u ? 0u : sbuf - 1u;       break; /* DEC sat  */
                case 5u: snew = ~sbuf & 0xffu;                     break; /* INVERT   */
                case 6u: snew = (sbuf + 1u) & 0xffu;               break; /* INC wrap */
                default: snew = (sbuf - 1u) & 0xffu;               break; /* DEC wrap */
            }
            snew = (sbuf & ~swm) | (snew & swm);
            if (snew != sbuf) {
                /* a straddled word is shared with the neighboring cell,
                   and a full-word read-modify-write could lose its
                   concurrent store */
                if (zua)
                    ust8(sba, snew);
                else
                    zw_st(zstg, w, (zw_ld(zstg, w) & ~(0xffu << ssw))
                                       | (snew << ssw));
            }
        }
        if (!sres || !zres)
            return;
    }

    uint dbpp  = (tris[ti].dst_dt == 6u) ? 4u : 2u;
    uint drw   = spans[si].drow;
    bool dstg  = (drw & 0x80000000u) != 0u;
    uint daddr = dstg ? ((drw & 0x7fffffffu) + uint(px) * dbpp)
                      : ((drw + uint(px) * dbpp) & pc.vram_mask);

    if (V_BLEND != 0 && !cdead) {
        uint          dst = dst_read(daddr, tris[ti].dst_dt, dstg);
        precise float dc[4], fs[4], fd[4];

        dc[0] = div255((dst >> 16) & 0xffu);
        dc[1] = div255((dst >> 8) & 0xffu);
        dc[2] = div255(dst & 0xffu);
        dc[3] = div255(dst >> 24);
        if (FOLDB(FB_BLEND, tris[ti].bsrc, 0xbu) == 0xbu || FOLDB(FB_BLEND, tris[ti].bsrc, 0xbu) == 0xcu) {
            precise float sa = (FOLDB(FB_BLEND, tris[ti].bsrc, 0xbu) == 0xbu) ? col[3] : 1.0 - col[3];

            fs[0] = fs[1] = fs[2] = fs[3] = sa;
            fd[0] = fd[1] = fd[2] = fd[3] = 1.0 - sa;
        } else {
            blend_factor(FOLDB(FB_BLEND, tris[ti].bsrc, 0xbu), col, dc, fs);
            blend_factor(FOLDB(FB_BLEND, tris[ti].bdst, 0xcu), col, dc, fd);
        }
        for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {
            precise float v = (FOLDB(FB_BLEND, tris[ti].bfcn, 0u) & 2u) != 0u
                            ? col[c] * fs[c] - dc[c] * fd[c]
                            : col[c] * fs[c] + dc[c] * fd[c];

            if ((FOLDB(FB_BLEND, tris[ti].bfcn, 0u) & 1u) != 0u)
                v = div255(uint(sat_i32(roundEven(v * 255.0)) & 0xff));
            else
                v = v < 0.0 ? 0.0 : (v > 1.0 ? 1.0 : v);
            col[c] = v;
        }
    }

    /* ---- pack, dither and plane-masked write ---- */
    if (!cdead) {
        uint outw = (sat_u32(col[3] * 255.0 + 0.5) << 24)
                  | (sat_u32(col[0] * 255.0 + 0.5) << 16)
                  | (sat_u32(col[1] * 255.0 + 0.5) << 8)
                  | sat_u32(col[2] * 255.0 + 0.5);
        uint bidx = uint(spans[si].py & 3) * 4u + uint(px & 3);

        dst_write(daddr, tris[ti].dst_dt, outw,
                  tris[ti].dither != 0u ? int(BAYER4(bidx)) : -1,
                  tris[ti].wmask, dstg);
    }

    /* Z writeback to the same cell the test read. At zbpp 4 the depth
       field is merged in and the stencil byte beside it kept. An aligned
       cell belongs to this pixel alone, so the read-modify-write cannot
       race; the s_atomic class, whose spans may overlap, never writes
       Z. A straddling cell merges all 4 bytes, reading its stencil byte
       again after the update above, and stores them with byte-lane
       atomics. */
    if (V_Z != 0 && tris[ti].z_wr != 0u) {
        /* With stencil folded off, nothing between the test and here
           uses the cell state, so it is computed again: the address,
           widths and depth depend only on the same tris, spans and lad
           inputs, and recomputing them means the compiler does not keep
           them in registers across the texture stages. When stencil can
           run (an unfolded pipeline or S_STEN 1), the state is live for
           the stencil update anyway and recomputing would only add work.
           The selector is a specialization constant, so only one arm is
           compiled. */
        uint wbpp, wshf, wmax, wcell, wzi;
        bool wstg, wua;

        if (C_FOLD != 0 && S_STEN == 0) {
            uint wrw = spans[si].zrow;

            wbpp  = tris[ti].zbpp;
            wshf  = tris[ti].zshift;
            wmax  = (wbpp == 4u) ? 0xffffffu : 0xffffu;
            wstg  = (wrw & 0x80000000u) != 0u;
            wcell = wstg ? ((wrw & 0x7fffffffu) + uint(px) * wbpp)
                         : ((wrw + uint(px) * wbpp) & pc.vram_mask);
            wua   = !wstg && (wcell & (wbpp - 1u)) != 0u;
            wzi   = lad[lat] & wmax;
        } else {
            wbpp = zbpp;  wshf = zshift; wmax = zmax; wstg = zstg;
            wcell = zcell; wua = zua;    wzi  = zi;
        }

        if (wbpp == 4u) {
            if (wua)
                ust32(wcell, (ldu32(wcell) & ~(wmax << wshf))
                                 | (wzi << wshf));
            else {
                uint w = wcell >> 2;

                zw_st(wstg, w, (zw_ld(wstg, w) & ~(wmax << wshf))
                                   | (wzi << wshf));
            }
        } else if (wua)
            ust16(wcell, wzi);
        else
            z16_st(wstg, wcell >> 1, wzi);
    }
}

void
main()
{
    uint g    = gl_WorkGroupID.x;
    uint bb   = rows[(pc.rows_base + g) * 2u];
    uint bc   = rows[(pc.rows_base + g) * 2u + 1u];
    uint sub  = gl_LocalInvocationID.x >> GPU_SLOT_SHIFT;
    uint lane = gl_LocalInvocationID.x & uint(GPU_SLOT_W - 1);

    if (V_SERIAL != 0) {
        /* one invocation: program order alone gives the interpreter's
           order, and no barriers run (the batch loop below is dead code
           under this constant) */
        if (gl_LocalInvocationID.x != 0u || g != 0u)
            return;
        for (uint k = 0u; k < bc; k++) {
            int si = int(bb + k);

            for (uint i = 0u; i < uint(spans[si].x1 - spans[si].x0 + 1); i++)
                pixel(si, spans[si].tri, i);
        }
        return;
    }

    for (uint k = 0u; k < bc; k++) {
        uint sb = (bb + k) * uint(GPU_BATCH_SLOTS);
        uint e  = slots[sb + sub];

        /* A batch that opens a level may touch bytes an earlier level
           wrote, so it waits at a barrier first. A batch split off only
           because the lanes ran out continues its level, whose spans are
           disjoint (or in the commutative stencil class), and needs no
           barrier. Bit 0 of the batch's first slot word carries the
           flag; pixel offsets are multiples of GPU_SLOT_W, so the decode
           below masks it off. Every invocation reads the same word, so
           the whole workgroup takes the same branch, as barrier()
           requires. */
        if (B_NOBAR == 0 && k > 0u && (slots[sb] & 1u) != 0u) {
            memoryBarrierBuffer();
            barrier();
        }
        if (e != 0xffffffffu) {
            int  si = int(e >> 8);
            uint i  = (e & uint(GPU_SLOT_MASK)) + lane;

            /* One pixel per invocation. A loop over several pixels per
               invocation runs slower in the gputri bench when texture
               and Z are both live: the extra registers lower the number
               of workgroups the GPU keeps resident. */
            if (i < uint(spans[si].x1 - spans[si].x0 + 1))
                pixel(si, spans[si].tri, i);
        }
    }
}
